WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:01.640
For memories to form,

00:00:01.720 --> 00:00:04.120
three functions have to take place.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:08.520
Acquiring - learning or experiencing
something new.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:13.480
Consolidating - this is where
the memories become stable.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:16.160
And finally, recall -

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:20.080
the ability to recall that
information in the future.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:34.040
Can juggling really increase
brain activity?

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:39.560
German researchers divided
24 non-jugglers into two groups

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:43.600
and asked one group to practise
juggling for three months.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:47.960
They found that those who didn’t
juggle showed no difference

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:50.640
in brain activity
over the test period.

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:56.200
But those who juggled, had an
increase in grey matter in two areas

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:59.920
of the hippocampus and neocortex,

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:05.760
parts of the brain involved in
memory, visual and motor activity.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:10.200
The brain is like a muscle
and needs exercise.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:12.480
To exercise your memory

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:16.640
you don’t necessarily have to learn
a new physical skill like juggling.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:19.200
(Speaking Spanish)

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:34.920
A memory palace is where
you imagine a place

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:36.800
where you can store information.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:40.360
Memory experts use this trick a lot
to remember number sequences

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:42.480
or even a whole deck of cards.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:46.320
Let's say you have a simple list
of things you want to buy.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:47.680
Step one:

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:51.040
Start at your door and go
anticlockwise around your room,

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:52.600
labelling each item

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:54.880
one to seven in order.

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:56.520
Step two:

00:01:56.600 --> 00:02:00.000
In my room,
my bed is the first object.

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:03.480
So I would place the first item
I want to remember -

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:05.040
buying an apple, for example -

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:06.400
on my bed.

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:08.200
Step three:

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:11.880
Create a visual image
and exaggerate it in some way

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:16.560
to make it more memorable.
If the first item is an apple,

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:20.560
picture a big purple apple
crushing your bed.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:24.720
Repeat the process until
every piece of information

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:28.160
is associated to an item
in your memory palace.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:37.400
During sleep, your body rests,
although your brain is busily

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:41.280
processing information from
the day to form memories.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:57.440
People who recall their dreams
improve their memory

00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:59.200
and are more creative.

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:02.160
Try keeping a dream diary
of some sort.

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:07.920
Just a notebook and pen, so you can
write down or sketch

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:11.480
as much as you can remember
as soon as you wake up.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:17.000
So there you have it. Acquiring,
consolidating, and finally recall.

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:19.080
Simple tips to improve your memory.

